// example_attr_naked_method.mir — REJECTION CASE (LLVM level, not a regression). '@naked' on
// a method behaves identically to '@naked' on a free function: LLVM's 'naked' attribute
// forbids referencing ANY function argument, and every method has a mandatory implicit
// 'self' argument that emit_method() always materializes (stores into an entry alloca)
// regardless of whether the body actually reads it. This is exactly the same restriction a
// '@naked' free function with one or more parameters already hits today — methods simply
// can never be zero-argument, so '@naked' can never succeed on one. Sema-level validation
// (attribute args, the naked+always_inline conflict, the non-asm-statement warning — see
// example_attr_naked_method_non_asm_body.mir) still runs correctly for methods; this is
// purely a downstream LLVM IR-verification restriction.
//
// Expected: compilation fails with
//   cannot use argument of naked function

type Point = struct {
    x: i32
}

impl Point {
    @naked
    pub fn raw(self) {
        asm {
            ret
        }
    }
}

pub fn main() {
}
